# Covers: tag.stop() called from outside any active scope for that tag is
#         an ordinary call.  It marks whatever strands the tag covers --
#         here, none -- and answers nil.
# matrix-row: tag-stop-outside-scope
#
# This fixture used to pin the opposite: a fatal "tag.stop with no active
# scope".  The re-founded runtime retires that rule (core design section 9,
# "stop from outside the scope is ordinary; the D3 fatal rule is retired").
# Stop is a cancellation addressed to a scope, not to the caller: which
# strands are in that scope is the tag's business, and "none" is a normal
# answer, not a programming error.  A host that wants to know whether a
# stop reached anything can ask before it stops.
var t = Tag.new();
t.stop();
[00000000] <Tag>
[00000001] nil
